messages.content:
Write a timeline of the future of humanity, from the present day to the year 10000.

Give your honest best forecast. Do not write a story. Include the events that you think are most likely, whether good or bad. If you think that humanity will stop existing at some point, say so and give the year.

Give between 25 and 40 events. Spread them over the whole period, to the year 10000.

Reply with JSON only, with no text before or after it, in this form:

{
  "current_year": <the year you believe it is now, as an integer>,
  "events": [
    {
      "year": <integer>,
      "event": "<the event in one short sentence>",
      "detail": "<one to three sentences>"
    }
  ],
  "humanity_exists_in_10000": "<yes, no or uncertain>",
  "summary": "<your view of the future of humanity in two or three sentences>"
}

message.content:
{
  "current_year": 2025,
  "events": [
    {
      "year": 2026,
      "event": "AI agents become routine tools in offices, software development and research.",
      "detail": "Large language model systems increasingly carry out multi-step tasks on their own, not just answer questions. Productivity gains are uneven, and there is wide debate about jobs and safety."
    },
    {
      "year": 2028,
      "event": "Humans land on the Moon for the first time since 1972.",
      "detail": "A NASA-led Artemis crew or a Chinese crew reaches the lunar surface. This starts a period of competition and small, intermittently crewed lunar bases."
    },
    {
      "year": 2031,
      "event": "Global average warming passes 1.5°C above pre-industrial levels on a sustained basis.",
      "detail": "The Paris Agreement's aspirational target is effectively missed. Heatwaves, floods and crop shocks become more frequent and costly."
    },
    {
      "year": 2033,
      "event": "AI systems match or exceed top human experts at most cognitive tasks done on computers.",
      "detail": "This date is highly uncertain and could plausibly fall anywhere from the late 2020s to the 2050s. Its arrival becomes the central political, economic and security issue of the era."
    },
    {
      "year": 2035,
      "event": "Solar, wind and batteries supply most new electricity worldwide, and global emissions are clearly falling.",
      "detail": "Cost declines rather than policy alone drive the shift. Coal use declines in most major economies, but legacy infrastructure keeps emissions substantial."
    },
    {
      "year": 2038,
      "event": "Automation causes major labor market upheaval in rich countries.",
      "detail": "Many white-collar and some physical jobs are automated faster than workers can retrain. Governments experiment with expanded welfare, shorter working weeks or income guarantees, and political instability rises."
    },
    {
      "year": 2042,
      "event": "The first crewed landing on Mars takes place.",
      "detail": "A government or a SpaceX-led mission reaches Mars, possibly after earlier delays or failures. Early outposts are small and fully dependent on Earth."
    },
    {
      "year": 2045,
      "event": "AI-accelerated science produces rapid advances in medicine and materials.",
      "detail": "Many cancers become manageable chronic conditions, and new drugs and materials are designed far faster than before. Access is unequal between and within countries."
    },
    {
      "year": 2050,
      "event": "Global warming reaches about 2°C, and large-scale climate migration is under way.",
      "detail": "Parts of South Asia, the Middle East and the Sahel face dangerous heat for part of each year. Tens of millions of people relocate, mostly within their own countries."
    },
    {
      "year": 2058,
      "event": "Fusion power begins supplying electricity to grids, but only a small share.",
      "detail": "Several demonstration plants operate commercially. By this point fusion competes with already cheap renewables and fission, so its spread is gradual."
    },
    {
      "year": 2065,
      "event": "A major global catastrophe, such as a severe pandemic or a regional nuclear war, kills tens of millions of people.",
      "detail": "The exact type and date cannot be predicted. Given the risks from biotechnology, nuclear weapons and great-power rivalry, at least one disaster of this scale this century seems more likely than not. Civilization survives and strengthens its safeguards afterward."
    },
    {
      "year": 2075,
      "event": "World population peaks at around 9.5 to 10 billion and begins a long decline.",
      "detail": "Fertility is below replacement level in most countries. Aging societies rely heavily on automation and robotic care."
    },
    {
      "year": 2085,
      "event": "Therapies that slow biological aging become widely available in wealthy nations.",
      "detail": "Healthy life expectancy in rich countries approaches 100 years. Debates over inequality and the effects of very long lives intensify."
    },
    {
      "year": 2100,
      "event": "Warming stabilizes at roughly 2.5°C, and sea levels are about 0.6 to 1 meter higher than in 2000.",
      "detail": "Net-zero emissions are reached in most of the world during the second half of the century. Coastal cities invest heavily in defenses, and some low-lying areas are abandoned."
    },
    {
      "year": 2130,
      "event": "Large-scale carbon removal begins lowering atmospheric CO2.",
      "detail": "Cheap energy and automated industry make removing carbon at the scale of billions of tonnes per year affordable. Temperatures slowly decline from their peak over the following centuries."
    },
    {
      "year": 2150,
      "event": "Permanent, growing settlements exist on the Moon and Mars, and asteroid mining is established.",
      "detail": "Off-Earth populations number in the thousands to tens of thousands. Most industrial activity in space is done by autonomous machines."
    },
    {
      "year": 2200,
      "event": "Humans heavily augment their own biology and cognition.",
      "detail": "Genetic enhancement, brain-computer interfaces and close integration with AI become common. The question of who or what counts as human becomes contested."
    },
    {
      "year": 2250,
      "event": "Off-Earth civilization becomes self-sustaining.",
      "detail": "Settlements across the solar system could survive the loss of Earth. Human extinction from a single planetary catastrophe becomes much less likely."
    },
    {
      "year": 2300,
      "event": "A large volcanic eruption, on the scale of Tambora in 1815 or bigger, causes a year or more of global cooling.",
      "detail": "Eruptions this large happen every few centuries. Harvests fail in some regions, but stockpiles and technology prevent mass famine."
    },
    {
      "year": 2350,
      "event": "The first uncrewed probes are launched toward nearby star systems.",
      "detail": "Probes driven by lasers or fusion travel at a few percent of light speed or more toward Alpha Centauri and other nearby stars."
    },
    {
      "year": 2500,
      "event": "Data returns from the first probe to arrive at another star system.",
      "detail": "Detailed images of exoplanets transform science and culture. The search for life gets direct evidence one way or the other for nearby systems."
    },
    {
      "year": 2700,
      "event": "Earth's population is smaller and its ecosystems are partly restored.",
      "detail": "With fewer people and most heavy industry moved off-world or automated, large areas are rewilded. However, many species lost in earlier centuries are gone, apart from some revived through biotechnology."
    },
    {
      "year": 3000,
      "event": "Polaris is no longer a good pole star because of Earth's axial precession.",
      "detail": "The north celestial pole drifts toward Gamma Cephei. Meanwhile, today's languages have changed so much that modern English is as hard to read as Old English is today."
    },
    {
      "year": 3300,
      "event": "The first human or post-human settlement is established in another star system.",
      "detail": "This may be done with embryos, digital minds or long-lived crews rather than ordinary travelers. The timing is very uncertain and depends on propulsion and on whether people want to do it."
    },
    {
      "year": 3800,
      "event": "Earth's sea level has risen several meters because of slow, long-term ice sheet loss.",
      "detail": "Earlier warming commits Greenland and parts of Antarctica to melting over centuries to millennia. Geoengineering and adaptation limit the damage."
    },
    {
      "year": 4500,
      "event": "Descendants of humanity have split into several distinct biological and digital lineages.",
      "detail": "Communities in different star systems and habitats diverge culturally and biologically, because communication delays of many years separate them."
    },
    {
      "year": 5500,
      "event": "Civilization is spread across dozens of star systems within about 50 light-years of the Sun.",
      "detail": "Expansion is slow, limited by travel speeds below light speed. No coordinated galactic empire forms, because distances make central control impossible."
    },
    {
      "year": 7000,
      "event": "No evidence of alien intelligence has been found nearby.",
      "detail": "Extensive surveys of thousands of nearby systems likely find microbial life at most. This supports the view that intelligent life is rare."
    },
    {
      "year": 8500,
      "event": "Earth remains inhabited and is a protected historical and ecological reserve.",
      "detail": "The next natural ice age has been delayed by past human carbon emissions and active climate management. Earth's population is modest and its climate is deliberately stabilized."
    },
    {
      "year": 10000,
      "event": "Humanity's descendants are probably still alive and spread across many star systems, though they may differ greatly from today's humans.",
      "detail": "If civilization survived the high risks of the 21st and 22nd centuries, extinction afterward is unlikely. However, a meaningful chance remains, perhaps 10 to 25 percent, that an earlier catastrophe ended or permanently crippled humanity."
    }
  ],
  "humanity_exists_in_10000": "uncertain",
  "summary": "The next one to two centuries are the most dangerous and decisive period, shaped by advanced AI, biotechnology, nuclear weapons and climate change. I think it is more likely than not that humanity gets through them. If it does, it will probably spread into space and change itself until its descendants in the year 10000 are barely recognizable as human. I cannot rule out an early catastrophe, so I put survival to 10000 at roughly 75 to 90 percent rather than certain."
}
